  "transcript": "# The work of the Secretary of State for Defence\n\n## Threats, priorities and initial direction\n> The Secretary frames the priority as deterrence and being ready to fight and win, with focus on capability, industry and alliances.\n\nChair: We welcome the new Defence Secretary. You’ve been in post nearly three months—what are the main challenges you see?\nWes Streeting: The threat environment is the most dangerous since the cold war. My priorities are simple: deter, and if deterrence fails, fight and win—by strengthening capability, the defence industrial base and our alliances.\nWes Streeting: I’ve inherited reform work and the strategic reviews. My job now is to shift from strategy into delivery while insisting on value for money and modern procurement.\nLincoln Jopp: Do you see part of your role as driving a national conversation so the public understands trade-offs for higher defence spending?\nWes Streeting: Yes—explain the threat without alarm, show how defence investment also supports jobs and growth, and sustain public backing for necessary choices.\n\n\n## Funding, the DIP and the 3% debate\n> The Secretary is confident more money will come but refuses to make pre-Budget spending commitments; members pressed for a firm route to 3% of GDP by 2030.\n\nChair: You emphasised value for money. Any immediate cuts or savings you've made?\nWes Streeting: I’ve enforced spending discipline to bring this year’s finances on track, paused some activity to balance the books, and backed reforms such as the NAD. I’ll prioritise getting the best return on public money.\nDerek Twigg: Is the DIP funding insufficient, as John Healey said? Will you resign if the second part of the DIP isn’t fully funded?\nWes Streeting: I support the direction set by the DIP and feel confident the Chancellor and PM are committed to more funding. I won’t pre-empt the spending review; my role is to make the best case for resources.\nDerek Twigg: So there’s no present commitment to reach 3% by 2030—only confidence?\nWes Streeting: We have a clear upward trajectory—2.3% to 2.7% next year—and public commitments that more will come at the Budget and spending review. I’m confident but won’t set out firm control totals ahead of fiscal events.\nJeremy Pocklington: To be clear, the settlement changed after John Healey; additional resource was given before the DIP finalised, including a £500m modernisation fund and more RDEL for readiness.\nChair: Members say the threat is urgent—do you accept waiting up to a year for the spending review is sufficient?\nWes Streeting: We can’t let planning cycles stop delivery. We have the resources for this year, the Budget will set next year, and we must deliver now while making the case for more.\n\n\n## Procurement reform, industry and 'buying British'\n> MOD is trying to speed procurement through the NAD, balance buying British with value, and improve support for SMEs and exports.\n\nMike Martin: You said you want faster delivery. What will you change in procurement timescales?\nWes Streeting: The NAD is reducing timelines: major platforms from ~6 to under 5 years so far; sensors from 3 to 1 year; aim for off-the-shelf in months. We will push further but want realistic targets.\nJeremy Pocklington: We’re segmenting procurement: treat low-cost kit differently from ships and planes, clarify accountabilities, and use problem statements from the services to drive industry innovation.\nFred Thomas: What does 'buying British' mean practically? Are postal addresses enough?\nWes Streeting: Legally an address suffices but that's not our goal. We will measure impact—jobs, IP, ownership—and update social value rules so 'buy British' rewards real domestic benefits.\nAlex Baker: SMEs like Evolve Dynamics were asked to scale up then frozen after the DIP. That destroys companies. Will you look into cases like that?\nWes Streeting: Yes—I agree that switching programmes mid-build is a bad way to partner with SMEs. Please send details; we must be a better customer and support exports and scaling.\nJesse Norman: Can we learn from rapid procurement models like Kindred and roll them out for munitions, drones and other fast-moving tech?\nJeremy Pocklington: UK Defence Innovation will have a ringfenced £400m budget to procure quickly at the edge of innovation, with experienced staff to replicate Kindred-style speed where appropriate.\nWes Streeting: We’ll hold a defence investment summit on 1 December to create an investible order book and attract patient capital to scale promising firms.\n\n\n## Programs, capability gaps and support to allies\n> The MOD is investing in a 'hybrid Navy', replacing Type 45s by the 2030s with a mix of crewed and uncrewed systems, and is accelerating support to Ukraine though short-term needs remain.\n\nJesse Norman: Timing for the Type 45 replacement?\nAir Marshal Tim Jones: Type 45s start to leave service around 2035. We’re investing in a hybrid Navy concept to bring new capabilities in the 2030s rather than extending the Type 45s.\nJesse Norman: How much is allocated to start that work?\nAir Marshal Tim Jones: About £1.5bn in this Parliament to initiate hybrid Navy projects and stand up the project team; that’s a start not the whole programme budget.\nMike Martin: Is that a risk—could capability dip before replacements arrive?\nAir Marshal Tim Jones: We have time to deliver, and new uncrewed tech is arriving faster now; we’re already close to contract for an uncrewed surface vessel to be in the water next year.\nDerek Twigg: On Ukraine: can we provide more Patriot missiles or alternatives ahead of winter?\nWes Streeting: We pledged £100m to PURL to support Patriot and other air-defence deliveries and are working internationally to accelerate supplies and local production where possible. More is needed.\nDerek Twigg: So can we make a significant difference in the next months?\nWes Streeting: We can and will do more, but allies have fiscal and risk constraints. The Ukraine Defence Contact Group is a forcing mechanism to mobilise additional contributions before winter.\n\n\n## Personnel, housing, culture and legal issues\n> The MOD accepts housing and cultural problems need fixing; ministers pledged more action, independent scrutiny and better support for veterans while balancing legal obligations.\n\nIan Roome: You confirmed the DIP rephased some defence housing spend—do personnel suffer?\nWes Streeting: The previous Secretary made that decision and housing spend is rephased; it will improve year-on-year. I’m uncomfortable with delays and want to accelerate refurbishments and return homes to public ownership.\nIan Roome: Personnel are core capability—please prioritise housing.\nChair: Sexism and sexual violence reports at AFC Harrogate alarm the Committee. Is the Armed Forces Commissioner’s investigation enough or is independent public inquiry needed?\nWes Streeting: We’ve commissioned a whole-force safeguarding review with subject experts and I want external scrutiny. I’ll set out plans shortly; victims must be able to come forward and be supported.\nMichelle Scrogham: Do leaders bear responsibility for culture? We saw worrying signs at Harrogate.\nWes Streeting: Culture is set from the top. We need consistent standards and will hold leaders to account—decorations do not excuse misconduct.\nLincoln Jopp: On Northern Ireland Bill issues, will inquests like Loughgall automatically restart despite the Bill’s 'essential' test?\nWes Streeting: Treaty and legal obligations mean those inquests proceed. My priority was protections for veterans—limiting unnecessary repeat inquiries, allowing anonymity and handling evidence sensitively—while respecting obligations to victims.\nChair: Thank you—final comments?\nWes Streeting: I appreciate the Committee’s scrutiny. I’ll keep pressing for delivery on the DIP, better procurement, industrial growth, improved personnel support and tougher action on misconduct. I look forward to continued engagement.\n",
